    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "High-throughput sequencing of PCR-amplified taxonomic markers\r\n(like the 16S rRNA gene) has enabled a new level of analysis of\r\ncomplex bacterial communities known as microbiomes.  Many tools exist\r\nto quantify and compare abundance levels or OTU composition of\r\ncommunities in different conditions.  The sequencing reads have to be\r\ndenoised and assigned to the closest taxa from a reference\r\ndatabase. Common approaches use a notion of 97\\% similarity and\r\nnormalize the data by subsampling to eqalize library sizes.  In this\r\npaper, we show that statistical models allow more accurate abundance\r\nestimates. By providing a complete workflow in R, we enable the user\r\nto do sophisticated downstream statistical analyses, whether\r\nparametric or non-parametric. We provide examples of using the R\r\npackages dada2, phyloseq, DESeq2 and vegan to filter, visualize and\r\ntest microbiome data and community networks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Supplementary Files to:\r\nBen J. Callahan, Kris Sankaran, Julia A. Fukuyama, Paul J. McMurdie, Susan P. Holmes,\r\nBioconductor workflow for microbiome data analysis: from raw reads to community analyses,\r\nF1000Research 2016, 5:1492 (doi: 10.12688/f1000research.8986.1).",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "displayLabel": "Preferred Citation",
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
